      "math_academy_id": "q-340367",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nGiven the inner product on the set of continuous functions over $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi )$ defined above, select the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$\n$⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,$\nThe functions $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ \n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "We begin by using the half-angle identity\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)=\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -(-\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{12}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-6\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ $\\text{are not orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product.",
